{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The defect\n\nThe scheduled **Terraform Drift** workflow runs `terraform fmt -check` as its FIRST step, before\n`terraform plan`. `fmt -check` exits non-zero on any formatting deviation, so a purely cosmetic\nwhitespace difference aborts the job and **`terraform plan` never executes**. The workflow's\nentire purpose — detecting infrastructure that has drifted from, or was never applied to match,\nthe declared configuration — is silently skipped.\n\nVERIFIED from run history on `main`:\n\n```\n2026-09-04T06:39 UTC  Terraform Drift  FAILURE  22s    (sha 6b034b9f)\n2026-09-03T06:40 UTC  Terraform Drift  FAILURE  29s    (sha 3e04025b)\n2026-09-02T06:44 UTC  Terraform Drift  success  6m07s\n```\n\nFailure log (run 33845247102):\n```\nplan  terraform fmt -check   monitoring_9ea3.tf\nplan  terraform fmt -check   ##[error]Process completed with exit code 3.\n```\n\nNote the durations: 22s and 29s versus 6m07s for the successful run. The short runtime is the\nsignature — the job died before doing any real work.\n\n## What it cost\n\nThis is not hypothetical. While drift detection was blinded, story S1 of epic 6202 landed code\ndeclaring a dedicated gate-scratch EBS volume, its attachment, and two CloudWatch alarms — and\n**none of them were ever applied**. The volume does not exist in AWS or in Terraform state; the\nlive metric `gate_scratch_mounted` has read 0 on all 51 datapoints since it began publishing,\nnever once 1. Gates continued writing scratch to the root filesystem, which is the exact state\nADR 0112 exists to prevent.\n\n**Drift detection is the control that would have reported those declared-but-unapplied\nresources, and a whitespace nit switched it off for two days.** The story closed green on code\nevidence in the meantime.\n\n## The general shape\n\nThis is the \"absence of execution reported as success/failure without anyone noticing WHAT did\nnot run\" class that has now recurred repeatedly in this repo (9a17's worker kill, 90c7's vacuous\nvote, 1ef8's vacuously-passing AC3, docker-storage-cap's SIGHUP reload reporting a cap \"in\neffect\"). Here the run did go red, so it was not falsely green — but it went red for a cosmetic\nreason that reads as trivial, and nothing distinguished \"formatting is off\" from \"we have not\nlooked for drift in 48 hours\".\n\n## Current status\n\nThe formatting fix has landed: `cad66dd237cc` \"Format monitoring_9ea3.tf to satisfy fmt gate\"\n(2026-09-04 09:54 UTC, present on origin/main). `terraform fmt -check -recursive infra/terraform`\nnow exits 0 locally. The next scheduled run (~2026-09-05 06:40 UTC) should clear fmt and reach\n`plan` — **but that has not yet been observed**, and when it does run it should report the two\nmissing gate-scratch resources as drift.\n\nSo the immediate blockage is cleared. This ticket is about the ORDERING, which is unchanged and\nwill do the same thing the next time any `.tf` file is misformatted.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] AC1 — A formatting-only defect in any `infra/terraform/*.tf` file no longer prevents\n      `terraform plan` from running in the Drift workflow. Prove it with a run (or a test\n      harness) in which `fmt -check` would fail and `plan` still executes and reports.\n- [x] AC2 — A formatting failure is still SURFACED, not silently dropped. Whatever the fix\n      (reorder, `continue-on-error` with an explicit later gate, split into independent jobs),\n      the run must still tell an operator that formatting is wrong.\n- [x] AC3 — Drift findings and formatting findings are DISTINGUISHABLE in the run's outcome, so\n      \"we did not look for drift\" can never again be indistinguishable from \"whitespace is\n      wrong\". A reader must be able to tell which occurred without opening logs.\n- [x] AC4 — A test or documented check pins AC1, so a future edit cannot silently restore the\n      blocking order.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nNot changing what drift is checked, not changing alarm or volume definitions, and not applying\nany Terraform — `terraform apply` is operator-only. This ticket is purely about the workflow's\nstep ordering and result legibility."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788589497790579001,"uuid":"8bde7455-137a-432e-adb1-6fce4c6924c4"}